# Alt Fun — Monorepo Cursor Rules

## Stack

npm workspaces monorepo with Turborepo. TypeScript strict mode everywhere.

- `apps/web/` — React 19, Vite 7, CSS Modules, Redux Toolkit, TanStack Query, Privy, viem, lightweight-charts
- `apps/api/` — Hono on Cloudflare Workers, Drizzle ORM, Neon (PostgreSQL), R2 (images), Durable Objects (WebSocket)
- `apps/indexer/` — Ponder (EVM indexer), GraphQL API auto-generated from schema
- `packages/contracts/` — Foundry (Solidity), forked from Virtuals Protocol
- `packages/shared/` — Shared types, ABIs (generated from Foundry), constants, contract addresses
- `packages/config/` — Shared ESLint and TypeScript configs

## Architecture

Contracts emit events -> Ponder indexes them into a GraphQL API (read path).
Hono API handles off-chain data: comments, user profiles, admin actions, API keys (write path).
Frontend queries Ponder for on-chain data, Hono API for off-chain data, and uses Privy for wallet interactions.
ABIs flow: Foundry build -> export script -> `packages/shared/src/abis/` -> consumed by all apps.

## Code Style

- TypeScript strict mode, never use `any` — use `unknown` with type guards
- Prefer named exports, except route modules and single-purpose utilities (use `export default`)
- Use early returns to reduce nesting
- No barrel exports (no re-exporting everything from index files beyond `packages/shared`)
- CSS Modules for all styling — never use Tailwind, styled-components, or inline styles
- No React class components — functional components with hooks only
- Import types with `import type` when only used as types
- Use `as const` on ABIs and constant objects for full type inference

## Comments

Default to no comment. Only add one when the code can't speak for itself.

- **Comment "why", not "what".** Security implications, non-obvious invariants, cross-system constraints (e.g. "must stay in sync with `vanity.ts`"), surprising trade-offs, footguns. Skip narration of what the next line literally does.
- **Don't restate the function/variable name.** A natspec that just rephrases `function setFeeTo(address feeTo_)` as "Set the fee-to address" adds nothing — drop it.
- **One source of truth.** If a struct/field already documents a rationale, don't repeat it at every use site. Link or stay silent.
- **Don't duplicate `AGENTS.md` / `docs/`.** Contract-level natspec should orient a reader, not re-derive what's already in the spec — link to it instead.
- **Section banners (`// ─── X ───`)** are fine in long files (>300 lines) where they help navigation; drop them in short files.
- **No "this is intentional" filler.** If a `try/catch` swallows by design, the natspec on the function should say so once; the body shouldn't repeat it.
- **`*.module.css` files: keep them silent.** Selector names already say what's being styled, and CSS rarely surfaces non-obvious *why*. Skip top-of-file rationale, `/* ─── Section ─── */` banners, and per-rule narration. Leave a comment **only** when something is genuinely surprising and the next reader would otherwise undo it (e.g. a browser-quirk workaround like `border-collapse: separate` being required for sticky `<thead>`). One terse line is enough — never multi-line rationale.
- Smart-contract comment guidance is stricter (audit-readability matters) — see [`packages/contracts/AGENTS.md`](packages/contracts/AGENTS.md#comment-style).

## Terminology

| Use this | Not this |
|---|---|
| leverage boost | HYPE boost, LT gain, leverage appreciation |
| bonding curve | curve, bond |
| graduation | migrate, launch |
| seed buy | dev buy, initial buy |
| curve filled | progress, bonding |
| HyperSwap | DEX (when referring to the specific post-grad venue) |
| LT (Leveraged Token) | leveraged token (always capitalise) |

## Task Tracking (MANDATORY)
`TODO.md` in the repo root is the single source of truth for open tasks.
- Before starting work, check `TODO.md` for relevant items.
- When completing a task listed there, remove it.
- When discovering new work that can't be done immediately, add it to `TODO.md`.

## CI Verification (MANDATORY)
After making any code changes, you MUST run the appropriate CI command from the repo root before responding. If it fails, fix it before responding. Never leave the user to discover CI failures after pushing.

The smart-contract test suite (`forge test`) is the slowest thing in CI by an order of magnitude, so we deliberately split local CI to match how GitHub's workflow splits its jobs (`js-checks` vs `contracts`):

| Command | Scope | Run when… |
|---|---|---|
| `npm run ci` | Lint + typecheck + test + build for every workspace **except** `@launchpad/contracts` | Default after any code change. Use this whenever your edits don't touch `.sol` files. |
| `npm run ci:contracts` | `forge fmt --check` + `forge test` (the contracts package only) | Run **in addition to** `npm run ci` whenever you've edited any file under `packages/contracts/`. |
| `npm run ci:full` | Everything above in one shot | Only when you've changed both contracts AND something else and want a single command. |

**Do not run `npm run ci:full` (or `npm run ci:contracts`) unless your changeset actually modifies smart-contract code.** Re-running the slow Foundry suite for a CSS tweak or an API edit wastes minutes per turn for no signal — neither GitHub CI nor any reviewer benefits from it. Turbo's cache will skip a re-run on subsequent invocations, but the first uncached run still pays the full cost.

If you're unsure whether your edits count as "smart-contract changes": the trigger is any `.sol` file under `packages/contracts/src/`, `packages/contracts/test/`, or `packages/contracts/script/`, plus `foundry.toml` and `remappings.txt`. Edits to `packages/contracts/AGENTS.md` or other non-Solidity files in that package do not require running `npm run ci:contracts`.

## Prohibitions

- Never use Tailwind CSS
- Never use `any` type
- Never hardcode secrets, API keys, or private keys
- Never modify `AGENTS.md` files or `docs/*.md` during code tasks without explicit instruction
- Never add marketing language to code comments or internal docs
- Never use `console.log` in production code — use structured logging
- Never commit `.env` files
